{"name":"Oyster Island","hex":"#efefe5","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"oyster","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of certain marine bivalve mollusks, especially those of the family Ostreidae (the true oysters), usually found adhering to rocks or other fixed objects in shallow water along the seacoasts, or in brackish water in the mouth of rivers.","The delicate oyster-shaped morsel of dark meat contained in a small cavity of the bone on each side of the lower part of the back of a fowl."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a pale beige colour tinted with grey or pink, like that of an oyster."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oyster"},{"term":"island","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A contiguous area of land, smaller than a continent, totally surrounded by water.","A contiguous area of land, smaller than a continent, partially surrounded by water; a peninsula; a half-island."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To surround with water; make into an island.","To set, dot (as if) with islands."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/island"}],"entries":[{"title":"Great Barrier Island","blocks":[{"text":"Great Barrier Island lies in the outer Hauraki Gulf, New Zealand, 100 kilometres (62 mi) north-east of central Auckland. With an area of 285 square kilometres (110 sq mi) it is the sixth-largest island of New Zealand. Its highest point, Mount Hobson, is 627 metres (2,057 ft) above sea level. The local authority is the Auckland Council."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Medlands_Beach_From_High_Up_West.jpg/330px-Medlands_Beach_From_High_Up_West.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Barrier_Island"},{"title":"Oyster","blocks":[{"text":"Oyster is the common name for a number of different families of salt-water bivalve molluscs that live in marine or brackish habitats. In some species, the valves are highly calcified, and many are somewhat irregular in shape. Many oysters are in the superfamily Ostreoidea."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Crassostrea_gigas_p1040848.jpg/330px-Crassostrea_gigas_p1040848.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oyster"},{"title":"Island","blocks":[{"text":"An island or isle is a piece of land, distinct from a continent, completely surrounded by water. There are continental islands, which were formed by being split from a continent by plate tectonics, and oceanic islands, which have never been part of a continent. Oceanic islands can be formed from volcanic activity, grow into atolls from coral reefs, and form from sediment along shorelines, creating barrier islands. River islands can also form from sediment and debris in rivers. Artificial islands are those made by humans, including small rocky outcroppings built out of lagoons and large-scale land reclamation projects used for development."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg/330px-Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Island"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Medlands_Beach_From_High_Up_West.jpg/500px-Medlands_Beach_From_High_Up_West.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Medlands_Beach_From_High_Up_West.jpg/1280px-Medlands_Beach_From_High_Up_West.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Medlands_Beach_From_High_Up_West.jpg/500px-Medlands_Beach_From_High_Up_West.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMedlands_Beach_From_High_Up_West.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Kauri_Dam_on_Great_Barrier_Island_1966.jpg/500px-Kauri_Dam_on_Great_Barrier_Island_1966.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Kauri_Dam_on_Great_Barrier_Island_1966.jpg/500px-Kauri_Dam_on_Great_Barrier_Island_1966.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A kauri driving dam on Great Barrier Island, 1967. Logging was one of the early industries on the island, and this dam provided enough water to drive kauri logs 16 km (10 mi) to the sea.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKauri_Dam_on_Great_Barrier_Island_1966.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Whangaparapara_Great_Barrier.jpg/500px-Whangaparapara_Great_Barrier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Whangaparapara_Great_Barrier.jpg/500px-Whangaparapara_Great_Barrier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The sawmill at Whangaparapara, c. 1910","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhangaparapara_Great_Barrier.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Great_Barrier_Island_Pigeon-Gram_stamp_1899.jpg/250px-Great_Barrier_Island_Pigeon-Gram_stamp_1899.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Great_Barrier_Island_Pigeon-Gram_stamp_1899.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Great_Barrier_Island_Pigeon-Gram_stamp_1899.jpg/250px-Great_Barrier_Island_Pigeon-Gram_stamp_1899.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thu